cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this computation, its proof or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "A22: enclosure and proof of every learning trajectory of a 216-cell spiking network"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Breuillaud"
    given-names: "Nicolas"
version: "4.4"
date-released: "2026-10-27"
license:
  - MIT
  - CC-BY-4.0
abstract: >-
  End-to-end propagation of the A22 spiking-network realization (18 layers x 12 lanes,
  128 input pulses, horizon 1116) with outward-rounded affine interval arithmetic over a box of
  device parameters, independent for each input port of each cell, and initial states. All 18
  layers complete with no forced decision; the independent A14 reference gains lie inside the
  computed enclosures for 648 of 648 boxes. A manual proof, under explicit execution assumptions,
  shows that the computation covers every admissible training trajectory and that the learned
  state is invariant for every future pulse sequence with gaps of at least 20, with exact
  transmission of the pulse word. Release 4.0-depth extends this to arbitrary depth for six
  prescribed realizations: the full training transient is certified through 68 layers (128 for a
  connected two-region case), the learned state enters a tail contract, and every lane of a medium
  has one common limiting duration per layer, identical for different upstream preparations of the
  same medium. Release 4.1 extends the tail to the full independent parameter box, proves a
  deterministic limiting speed for stationary ergodic random media (Kingman's subadditive ergodic
  theorem), and gives speed ceilings for tail cohorts and for first activation during learning.
  Release 4.2 widens the tail contract and gives partial results, certified obstructions and a
  precise open problem for entry from every preparation of the original source.
  Release 4.3 adds a conditional relative-gain-domain future-invariance theorem, with 35 of 40
  declared decimal states passing directed membership checks; universal training entry remains open.
  The proof is not yet independently reviewed or machine-checked.
  The code, diagnostics and proof were produced with the AI assistants Claude (Anthropic) and
  ChatGPT (OpenAI) under the author's direction. The physical kernel is inspired by the author's
  Reset Operator.
keywords:
  - interval arithmetic
  - affine arithmetic
  - hybrid systems
  - validated numerics
  - spiking networks
  - computer-assisted proof
references:
  - type: unpublished
    title: "The Reset Operator: A Structural Constraint on Recursivity"
